Finding 013
Title
CODE Score Relationships Are Not Stable Across Route Drills

Date
2026-10-14

Context
During Milestone 03, the objective shifted from feature engineering to validation.

The initial analysis focused on the drill:

POST_CORNER_ROUTE_RIGHT
where tracking-derived metrics showed a modest positive relationship with NFL career outcomes.

To evaluate whether this relationship generalized beyond a single drill, the complete feature-engineering pipeline was replicated on a second route:

COMEBACK_ROUTE_RIGHT
The methodology, feature definitions, aggregation process, and validation approach remained unchanged.

Route Datasets
POST_CORNER_ROUTE_RIGHT
110 route attempts
95 players
COMEBACK_ROUTE_RIGHT
109 route attempts
97 players
Both drills had comparable sample sizes and therefore provide a useful cross-drill validation framework.

Metric Evaluated
Experimental metric:

CODE Score V3
Defined as:

direction_change_z +
path_efficiency_z
The score was designed to reward route attempts that simultaneously exhibited:

substantial directional change
efficient route geometry
The metric remains experimental and had not yet been validated against NFL outcomes.

Results
POST_CORNER_ROUTE_RIGHT
Relationship with NFL career outcomes:

CODE ↔ career_games_started

r ≈ +0.26
CODE ↔ career_offensive_snaps

r ≈ +0.24
Initial interpretation:

Higher CODE scores appeared weakly associated with greater NFL usage.

COMEBACK_ROUTE_RIGHT
Relationship with NFL career outcomes:

CODE ↔ career_games_started

r ≈ -0.29
CODE ↔ career_offensive_snaps

r ≈ -0.31
Interpretation:

The direction of the relationship reversed.

Players with higher CODE scores in this drill were associated with fewer career starts and fewer offensive snaps.

Key Observation
The relationship observed in POST_CORNER_ROUTE_RIGHT did not replicate in COMEBACK_ROUTE_RIGHT.

Not only did the magnitude change, but the sign of the relationship changed as well:

Positive → Negative
This is a stronger result than simply observing weaker correlations.

The finding suggests that route-dependent effects may be present.

Possible Explanations
Hypothesis A
CODE Score V3 is not a robust metric.

The initial positive relationship observed in POST_CORNER_ROUTE_RIGHT may have been driven by noise or drill-specific characteristics.

Hypothesis B
Different route drills measure fundamentally different athletic skills.

POST_CORNER_ROUTE_RIGHT emphasizes:

acceleration
route manipulation
spatial control
multi-stage directional transitions
COMEBACK_ROUTE_RIGHT emphasizes:

deceleration
braking ability
transition efficiency
change of momentum
The same tracking metric may therefore represent different underlying behaviors depending on the route context.

Hypothesis C
A universal route-execution score may not exist.

Different route families may require separate scoring frameworks.

In this interpretation:

Route Execution
would not be a single dimension but a collection of route-specific skills.

Implications
This finding challenges the assumption that a single composite tracking score can be applied uniformly across route types.

Future work should prioritize:

Route-specific analysis.
Validation across multiple drills.
Decomposition into interpretable dimensions.
Evaluation of Burst, Agility, and Efficiency separately.
Lessons Learned
The objective of validation is not to confirm hypotheses but to test them.

Although the result did not support the original expectation, it produced useful evidence:

The predictive behavior of tracking-derived metrics appears to depend on route context.

This insight may ultimately be more valuable than the original positive correlation observed in a single drill.

Conclusion
The initial relationship between CODE Score V3 and NFL career outcomes did not generalize across drills.

Evidence from COMEBACK_ROUTE_RIGHT suggests that route context plays a critical role in determining the usefulness of tracking-derived metrics.

At this stage, a route-specific evaluation framework appears more promising than the pursuit of a universal route-execution score.

Status
🟡 Validation In Progress

The original hypothesis has not been confirmed.

Further analysis across additional drills is required before drawing general conclusions about route-execution metrics and NFL success.
